  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis: Canon (web)\n\nI read through the server data layer (`lib/server/*`, `lib/db/*`), the client components (conflict queue, run polling, visual effects), and the shared utility libs. Headline finding: **this codebase is already unusually disciplined about performance** — the server layer has explicit comments about avoiding N+1s with grouped queries, nearly everything is a Server Component reading Postgres directly, and every animation/timer effect I checked (`GradientWaves`, `LogoLoop`, `TextType`, `RunLive`) cleans up correctly. I did not fabricate problems to pad this out. Below are the real, verified opportunities, ranked by impact.\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — none found; one duplicate-fetch risk to watch\n\nThe query layer (`lib/server/conflicts.ts`, `lib/server/export.ts`, `lib/server/entities.ts`) is deliberately batched — e.g. `getQueuePage` in `conflicts.ts:172-200` explicitly loads child-counts for a whole page in one grouped `GROUP BY` query instead of per-row, with a comment calling this out. No loop-issuing-a-query-per-row pattern exists in the routes I checked.\n\nThe one thing worth flagging: `countSourcesBySide()` (`lib/server/sources.ts:24-31`) calls `listSources()` internally, doing a fresh `SELECT` rather than reusing a result. It's currently only ever called alone (`app/get-started/page.tsx`), so there's no live duplicate query today — but if a future page calls both `listSources()` and `countSourcesBySide()` in the same render, that's two round trips for identical data. Cheap insurance:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide(all?: Source[]) {\n  const sources = all ?? (await listSources());\n  return {\n    crm: sources.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: sources.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: sources.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Unnecessary React re-renders — the conflict queue rows\n\n**This is the most actionable finding.** `ConflictQueue` (`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx`) holds `selectedIndex` and `overrideConflictId` in state, and renders up to `QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE = 100` rows (`types/queue.ts:134`). None of the row components (`ConflictDenseTable`, `ConflictDiffRow`) are wrapped in `React.memo`, and each row is handed a freshly-created closure every render:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx:174-186 — new arrow fn per row, every render\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    onSelect={() => setSelectedIndex(index)}\n    submit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}               // new closure per row\n    onOverrideOpenChange={(open) => setOverrideOpen(row.conflictId, open)}\n    ...\n  />\n))}\n```\n\nBecause nothing is memoized, pressing `j`/`k` to move the selection by one row — the single most common interaction in this UI — re-renders all 100 rows on every keystroke, even though only two rows' visual state (previously selected, newly selected) actually changed.\n\nFix: memoize the row components and pass stable, index-free callbacks (bind the id via a data attribute + a single stable handler, or `useCallback` keyed by id via a `Map` of memoized closures):\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-dense-table.tsx / conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ ... }) {\n  ...\n});\n```\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx — one stable handler instead of one closure per row\nconst handleRowSelect = useCallback((index: number) => setSelectedIndex(index), []);\nconst handleOverrideToggle = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string, open: boolean) => setOverrideOpen(conflictId, open),\n  [setOverrideOpen],\n);\n// pass row index/id down, call handleRowSelect(index) from inside the row via a\n// pre-bound prop, or delegate the click at the list level and read data-index\n// off event.currentTarget instead of allocating a closure per row.\n```\n\nCombined with `React.memo`, this turns a 100-row re-render into a 2-row re-render on selection change. For 400+ conflict runs (mentioned throughout the codebase's own comments) this is where a reviewer would actually feel lag.\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\nThe TanStack Query setup (`components/query-provider.tsx`) is intentionally scoped to run-status polling only, with `staleTime: 0` — that's correct given it's a 2s poll on a single value, not a caching gap.\n\nOne real opportunity: `getRuleset(run.rulesetId)` (`lib/server/rulesets.ts:44`) is read on every `loadQueue()` call (`lib/server/conflicts.ts:226`), i.e. once per conflicts-page render — including every pagination click and filter change. Rulesets are immutable once versioned (the codebase's own comment: \"never edited in place\"), so this is a textbook case for React's per-request memoization if it's ever called more than once per request, and a good candidate for a short-TTL cache across requests since a given `rulesetId` never changes its content:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/rulesets.ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const getRuleset = cache(async (id: string): Promise<Ruleset | null> => {\n  await connection();\n  const [row] = await getDb().select().from(rulesets).where(eq(rulesets.id, id)).limit(1);\n  return row ? rulesetSchema.parse(row) : null;\n});\n```\n`react`'s `cache()` dedupes calls within a single request for free — worth applying to `getRuleset`, `getRunDetail`, and `getSource` generally, since page + layout + nested Server Components sometimes read the same row independently (I didn't find a live double-call today, but it's the kind of thing that regresses silently as pages grow).\n\n### 4. Memory leaks — none found\n\nChecked every component with a `useEffect` that starts a timer/observer/listener:\n- `RunLive`'s `Elapsed` clock (`components/runs/run-live.tsx:98-118`) — clears `setInterval` correctly, guarded by `running`.\n- `GradientWaves` (WebGL/ogl) — cancels the animation frame, disconnects the `ResizeObserver`, removes the `webglcontextlost` listener, and explicitly loses the GL context on unmount. This is better cleanup than most production code.\n- `LogoLoop` — disconnects its `ResizeObserver`.\n- `TextType` — clears its `setTimeout` chain.\n- `ConflictQueue`'s `keydown` listener — removed on unmount.\n\nNo dangling subscriptions, no missing cleanup functions, no `setInterval` without a paired `clearInterval`.\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\nTwo small but genuine ones:\n\n**a) `foldValue()` re-sorts a constant array on every call** (`lib/validate-value.ts:64`):\n```ts\nexport function foldValue(value: string): string {\n  let folded = value.normalize(\"NFKD\")...;\n  const suffixes = [...LEGAL_SUFFIXES].sort((a, b) => b.length - a.length); // recomputed every call\n  ...\n}\n```\nThis runs on every keystroke in the override editor (\"the override editor runs it as you type\" per the file's own comment) and again per candidate in `checkObservedValue`'s `.find()` loop. The sort result never changes — hoist it:\n```ts\nconst SUFFIXES_BY_LENGTH: readonly string[] =\n  [...LEGAL_SUFFIXES].sort((a, b) => b.length - a.length);\n\nexport function foldValue(value: string): string {\n  let folded = value.normalize(\"NFKD\")...;\n  // use SUFFIXES_BY_LENGTH instead of resorting\n}\n```\n\n**b) `RulesetEditor` does two `indexOf` scans per row** (`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:166-179`):\n```tsx\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);          // O(n) scan\n  return (\n    <RuleRow\n      position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1}    // another O(n) scan\n      ...\n```\nThis makes rendering the rule table O(n²). Ruleset sizes are small today so it's not felt, but it's needless — `orderRulesByEvaluation` could return `{ rule, originalIndex }` pairs, or you can build an id→index map once before mapping:\n```tsx\nconst originalIndex = new Map(rules.map((r, i) => [r.id, i]));\n{ordered.map((rule, position) => (\n  <RuleRow key={rule.id} rule={rule} position={position + 1}\n    onChange={(patch) => update(originalIndex.get(rule.id)!, patch)} ... />\n))}\n```\n\n### Summary\n\n| Area | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| N+1 queries | None found — server layer is explicitly batched |\n| Re-renders | Real issue: unmemoized 100-row conflict list re-renders fully on every keyboard nav |\n| Caching | Minor gap: `getRuleset`/`getRunDetail` aren't request-deduped via `react.cache()` |\n| Memory leaks | None found — every effect cleans up correctly |\n| Redundant computation | Two small, real ones: `foldValue`'s array re-sort, `RulesetEditor`'s O(n²) `indexOf` |\n\nThe highest-value fix here is #2 (memoize the conflict queue rows) — it's the only one with a user-visible cost at realistic scale (a 400-conflict run, which the codebase's own comments treat as the target case).\n",
